Ulcerative Proctitis (Mild Distal IBD)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Occasional rectal bleeding, tenesmus, highly responsive to topical mesalazine

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-047
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of rectal bleeding and severe pelvic cramping (tenesmus) requiring exclusion of severe lower gastrointestinal hemorrhage.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute, repeated episodes of rectal bleeding accompanied by severe urgency. Immediate clinical review was required to rule out hemodynamically significant massive lower GI bleeding or an acute perirectal abscess prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I've been to the toilet six times in the last hour, passing mostly bright red blood and mucus. The cramping is agonizing, and I feel like I constantly need to go but nothing comes out.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a history of inflammation limited to the rectum. Tonight, the patient experienced an acute flare of severe tenesmus (constant feeling of needing to pass stool) and passed multiple small volumes of bright red blood mixed with mucus. The sudden increase in visible blood caused extreme distress and fear of exsanguination. Patient feels mildly lightheaded from panic. Denies large-volume dark blood clots, vomiting, fevers, or severe abdominal rigidity.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently unable to leave the proximity of the bathroom.
Social/Safety Concerns: Low risk.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 74 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused, in moderate discomfort from pelvic cramping, overtly anxious.
Physical Exam: Abdominal exam reveals a soft, non-distended abdomen with mild suprapubic tenderness but absolutely no guarding, rigidity, or rebound tenderness. Bowel sounds are normal. Visual inspection of the perianal area reveals no massive external hemorrhoidal bleeding or fluctuant abscess. Cardiovascular exam shows normal capillary refill and no postural blood pressure drop. History confirms occasional rectal bleeding, tenesmus, highly responsive to topical mesalazine.
Environmental Observations: Bathroom accessible, safe layout.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient ran out of mesalazine suppositories two weeks ago.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute flare of Ulcerative Proctitis (Mild Distal IBD) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Massive Lower GI Bleed (e.g., Diverticular Bleed) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal hemodynamics, absence of postural drop, and passage of small amounts of blood/mucus (classic proctitis) rather than painless, massive, large-volume dark bleeding.

Acute Perirectal Abscess - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Afebrile status, and lack of a severe, localized, exquisitely tender fluctuant mass near the anus.

Infectious Colitis (e.g., Campylobacter) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of fever, no widespread abdominal pain or large-volume watery diarrhea, and classic isolated tenesmus.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, frequent rectal bleeding and severe tenesmus, raising immediate clinical concern for a significant lower GI hemorrhage. Triage appropriately mandated immediate physical evaluation. Bedside assessment definitively established that the patient was hemodynamically stable without signs of massive blood loss or an acute surgical abdomen. The clinical picture perfectly matched an acute exacerbation of known distal proctitis secondary to medication non-compliance. This prompt clinical evaluation safely averted a highly distressed, unnecessary emergency department presentation for suspected severe hemorrhage, entirely justifying urgent after-hours care.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance provided regarding hydration and blood volume status. Issued a script for mesalazine suppositories/enemas to be commenced immediately.
Positive Behavioral Change: Insert the prescribed suppository immediately before going to sleep and remain lying on your left side. By keeping the medication in direct contact with the inflamed rectal lining overnight, the bleeding and the agonizing urge to go to the toilet will be drastically reduced by morning.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP or gastroenterology appointment in 7 days to ensure bleeding has ceased and to arrange maintenance scripts.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if you begin passing large, cup-sized volumes of dark red blood or clots, if you feel dizzy and actually faint when standing up, or if you develop a high fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive regarding the exact extent of the flare; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The lowest part of your bowel is severely inflamed, which causes it to bleed easily and falsely signal to your brain that it is full (tenesmus). Topical medication heals this very effectively.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if you develop severe, constant, sharp pain across your entire stomach that makes it hard to breathe.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A small amount of blood mixed with mucus in the toilet water looks much worse than it actually is. You have not lost a dangerous amount of blood. The suppository acts like a direct soothing bandage on the inflamed area.
Actionable Steps: You must adhere strictly to the suppository prescription even after the bleeding stops. Use a heat pack on your lower stomach tonight to ease the cramping.

SMS INFORMATION

Dx: Flare of Ulcerative Proctitis. Exam confirms NO dangerous blood loss. Hemodynamics stable. Mesalazine suppositories prescribed. See GP in 7 days.

SMS EDUCATION

Use the suppository before bed and lie on your left side. Go to the ED if you pass large cups of blood, faint, or get a high fever.
